The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471/) says “one issue, one fix queued.” Its own
rendered data disagrees. This review uses the complete 2026-10-04 export, deduplicates report
IDs, and reads every tenant's locale and booking form. The original pages, components and
accepted decision remain intact as historical evidence, not as the conclusion of this review.

## Latest desk update — closed, with different outcomes

<EvidenceReview section="outcomes" />

The desk has now reported the parser fix and retired the booking-v3 form instead of fixing
its hint. Those are different dispositions: **completed** for the parser issue, **not planned**
for the hint issue. Retired is not repaired, and closing a ticket is not evidence that every
symptom in it was reproduced or explained. The linked issues retain their historical counts
and the unresolved observations below. This repository records the desk's update; it does not
contain or claim to have tested the production fix.

## Problems filed separately

<EvidenceReview section="summary" />

**Parser:** in the exported incident, the shared parser read month first, while these tenants
wrote day first. App and email reports, plus booking-v2 form reports, follow that assignment.
A booking-v3 tenant's app/email report still follows the parser path.

**Hint:** booking-v3 printed `MM/DD/YYYY`, but its form read the date the tenant's day-first
way. People could follow the hint and still get the wrong date. A shared-parser fix alone
would not align that hint; the later desk decision retired the form instead.

These are disjoint filing buckets computed by `causeOf`, not two production fixes demonstrated
in this repository. The export contains no runnable booking service or production parser.

## Keep the unresolved observations visible

<Callout kind="warn">
A tenant-based filing rule does not explain every symptom. Do not promise that a parser fix
resolves one-day moves, timezone-dependent displays, or an invite allegedly arriving before
its booking. None of those diagnoses has been reproduced from this export.
</Callout>

<EvidenceReview section="flags" />

The desk's note calls this subset `oneDayMove`, but an ordering allegation is not itself a
measured one-day shift. Lisbon versus Toronto suggests timezone context worth collecting,
not proof of a timezone or daylight-saving defect. Obtain original booking/invite timestamps,
input values, tenant settings and display timezones; establish a reproducible failure before
splitting another cause. If that evidence changes an assignment, move its report IDs and
recompute the issue counts instead of double-counting them.

## Check every tenant, not the reporter's browser

The platform's locale date-part order determines day-first behavior. A form report belongs to
the hint bucket only when the tenant is day-first and runs booking-v3. Missing or non-day-first
tenants remain unplaced; do not force them into either known cause.

<EvidenceReview section="tenants" />

## The queue did not start in July

Use `receivedAt` for when the desk heard about a problem, and `mergedAt` for when it processed
the report into this ticket. The chart deduplicates IDs and buckets those timestamps separately
into Monday–Sunday weeks. Its range here is derived from the data, including the latest merge.

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} from={timelineFrom} to={timelineTo} />

<EvidenceReview section="months" />

Continued reports and the final reporter's “I have stopped reporting these” also mean the
exported count is not a count of everyone affected. The desk's later closure is recorded
above; this export supplies no post-fix verification of the individually flagged symptoms.

## Merge on established cause, not subject

The [subject-only triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe/) follows
[accepted decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject/), reviewed in 2024. Its stale
banner is meaningful: the examples below show both false merges and missed duplicates.
The same tenant can have different causes by channel; different tenants and subjects can
have the same cause. Two unknown senders do not establish the same cause.

<DedupeExplorer />

[Proposed decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause/) replaces subject-only matching
with an evidence-based recommendation. It is proposed, not a claim that the desk has adopted
it: read the body, inspect tenant locale/form/channel, and merge only when an established
cause matches. Keep unresolved evidence separate from certainty; preserve IDs and the original
arrival time. A ticket being marked “known” does not prove every report in it is explained.

## What the original component shows

This is the unchanged export component. Its “no ticket of its own” column describes the
export's historical ticket state; the new issue links above are the actual split filed by this
review. Its footnote deliberately retains the flagged reports inside the parser bucket.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Keep the desk's next changes honest

Reports, notes and tenant files feed the components; eager imports are a snapshot, not a live
desk subscription. Rebuild when a newer export arrives. Counts, group membership, timeline
range, warning rows and explorer verdicts are recomputed from those inputs. Review anomalous
reports rather than treating the classifier as exhaustive root-cause proof. Update the linked
GitHub issue counts when the filing changes, and review this page again; its stale warning
appears after the shared review-age threshold. Never rewrite an accepted decision record.

The machine-readable [render evidence](/evidence.json) contains every assigned report ID.
This page's desk reference below is computed from its frontmatter signature, not typed by hand.

